---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const SampleOutput = `
--- Order Summary ---
Item: Mechanical Keyboard
Quantity: 2
Unit price: 89.99
Line total: 179.98

In stock: true
`;

const Starter = `let itemName = "Mechanical Keyboard";
let quantity = 2;
let unitPrice = 89.99;
let inStock = true;

// Your code goes below this line.
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Receipt Line</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Store the details of a single order in variables, then print them as
            a readable summary. Every value on the receipt comes from a
            variable &mdash; not one of them is typed directly into a{" "}
            <code>console.log()</code> call.
          </p>

          <p>
            That restriction is the point. A program where the data lives in
            variables can be changed in one place; a program where the data is
            baked into the output has to be rewritten.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Declaring variables with <code>let</code></li>
            <li>Choosing the right data type for each value</li>
            <li>camelCase naming</li>
            <li>Printing a label and a variable together</li>
            <li>Calculating with variables rather than literal numbers</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="starter">
          <h2>Start From This</h2>

          <p>
            Copy these four lines into your program exactly as written, then
            build the rest below them.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Starter}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Receipt Line"
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Notice that three different data types are already in use here.
            Before you write anything, name them: which variable holds a string,
            which holds a number, and which holds a boolean?
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Tasks</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Create a new program named{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-receipt-line</b>.
            </li>
            <li>Print the heading line shown in the sample output.</li>
            <li>
              Print the item name, the quantity, the unit price, and whether it
              is in stock &mdash; each on its own line, each with a label.
            </li>
            <li>
              Declare a new variable named <code>lineTotal</code> and set it to
              the quantity times the unit price. <b>Do not type 179.98
              anywhere.</b>
            </li>
            <li>Print the line total.</li>
            <li>Print a blank line before the in-stock line.</li>
          </ul>

          <h5>Then prove it works</h5>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Change <code>quantity</code> to 5 and run the program again.
              The line total must update on its own.
            </li>
            <li>
              If it did not, your line total is a typed-in number rather than a
              calculation. Fix it before you submit.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h5>Challenge (optional)</h5>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a variable for a discount amount and print a second total with
              the discount subtracted. Keep the original line total on the
              receipt as well.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The line total shows as <code>179.98</code> here because 2 &times;
            89.99 happens to come out clean. You are not being asked to control
            the number of decimal places yet.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your program file, plus a one-line answer to this:</p>

          <p>
            <b>
              What was on the screen after you changed the quantity to 5?
            </b>{" "}
            Give the line total you saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            That number tells me whether your receipt is calculating or just
            reciting, which is the entire difference between this challenge and
            the last one.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
